WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:06.470
MODERATOR: Welcome. Thank you all, and I know
you're all really interested in Ukraine because

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:15.009
you have waited out the weather over schedules
and cancellations and here&nbsp;we finally are.

00:00:15.009 --> 00:00:19.990
And of course&nbsp;a lot of&nbsp;things have happened
even since we were originally scheduled to

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:25.869
do this.
Welcome&nbsp;to the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:31.599
You all&nbsp;I think&nbsp;know the rules which are
to turn off your cell phones, and this session

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:39.370
will be on the record. I will make some introductory
remarks, talk to our panelists a bit, and

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:44.070
then open the floor for questions. And there
will be further instructions before the questions

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:51.810
start. And we will end promptly at 1:30.
Our subject today is "Inside Ukraine." And

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:57.980
obviously, that's a very timely topic. I think
we couldn't have two better panelists to talk

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:04.070
to us. Not only about what's happening inside
Ukraine, what should happen, what the future

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:10.160
may bring, and, equally important, what's
going on inside Russia. You have their bios

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:15.200
in the papers that you were handed. But let
me just give you some high points.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.980
Ambassador John Herbst to my immediate right
is director of the Eurasia Center at the Atlantic

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:26.030
Council, with law experience and extensive
research&nbsp;and writing in Central Asia, Ukraine,

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:31.710
and Russia, as well as distinguished diplomatic
service in the region, including as Ambassador

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:38.310
to Ukraine from 2003 to 2006.
Matthew Rojansky is Director of the Kennan

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:43.550
Institute at the Wilson Center, and he was&nbsp;the
founder of the Ukraine program at Carnegie.

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:48.740
He's an expert on U.S. relations with the
states of the former Soviet Union, especially

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:54.969
Russia and Ukraine. He too has extensive experience
in the region and has written a great deal

00:01:54.969 --> 00:01:58.939
on it, which I'm sure you're all familiar
with.

00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:05.200
Before we hear from them, I just thought I'd
give you a brief update on the latest events

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.850
regarding Ukraine. As you know, last month's
recommitment to the Minsk Agreement has only

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:17.390
been partially implemented. And I use the
word "partially" euphemistically. While there

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:23.731
has been more or less a ceasefire and some
movement of heavy weapons in some areas, fighting

00:02:23.731 --> 00:02:29.360
has continued. The United States and its allies
and the United Nations have charged a continuing

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:35.830
flow of weapons across the Russian border
to the Russian-backed separatists.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:41.930
After he met this morning in Geneva with Secretary
Kerry, Russian Foreign Minister Lavrov said

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:46.900
that weapons were being withdrawn from the
front lines. As his government has said many

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:52.220
times, he called on Ukraine to distance itself
from what he called "nationalist extremists

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:58.290
in its ranks," and to get to work on promised
political and economic reforms for the Eastern

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:01.940
part of the country.
Secretary of State Kerry has just finished

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:10.220
a news conference in which he said that the
Russians had cherrypicked the areas in which

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:16.360
there had been an actual ceasefire and weapons
had been withdrawn. He, as you know, last

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:23.070
week accused Russia of lying about its support
for the separatists. This weekend, he accused

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:27.590
Russia of what he called "craven behavior"
and&nbsp;"undermining the ceasefire."

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:34.410
He also said he spoke with Foreign Minister
Lavrov about further sanctions, and said that

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:39.680
he thought that there had to be something
substantive that everybody could agree on

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.620
happening—he said it two different ways.
First he said "in a matter of hours". And

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:50.090
then later he said "in a matter of days".
The leaders of Ukraine, Russia, Germany, and

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:54.840
France, who negotiated both of the Minsk Accords
have scheduled a telephone conference later

00:03:54.840 --> 00:04:02.180
today to talk about its status and—to talk
as well about a new gas dispute between Russia

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:07.030
and Ukraine. Ukraine says that Russia's been
violating the agreement on gas they signed

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:13.000
last fall. And the Russian energy minister
is due to speak this afternoon with the EU

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.980
energy chief.
Meanwhile, as you all probably know, the United

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:23.000
Nations today issued a report in Geneva saying
that there were credible reports indicating

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.450
a continuing influx of heavy weapons from
Russia into the Donetsk and Luhansk regions.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:34.970
The report estimated that more than 6,000
people have been killed since last April,

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:40.090
and that from mid-January to mid-February
alone this year, at least 842 people were

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:46.790
killed and 3,400 were injured.
As we all know, President Obama has said that

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:52.980
he's weighing a decision on whether to supply
the Ukrainian military with at least defensive

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:57.570
lethal weapons that have been requested by
Ukraine.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:03.370
I wanted to start by asking Ambassador Herbst,
you were among the group of notables who recently

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:09.270
urged the President to make that decision
as a necessary means of restraining Putin

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:15.940
and Russia. Obama said he wanted to wait until
he saw the outcome of the newest iteration

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:20.280
of the Minsk Agreement. Do you think he's
waited long enough, and is it time to move?

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:27.930
HERBST: He's—he's waited far too long. The
ceasefire was violated overwhelmingly by the

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:31.430
Russian side, not simply the separatist side.
And, in fact, it's a fiction to talk about

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:36.510
them as if they're two separate entities.
The war in Ukraine is led by, financed by,

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:42.810
equipped by Russians, and there are substantial
Russian troops in Ukraine.

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:48.770
The ceasefire, according to the quote, unquote,
separatists does not apply to the one area

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:53.490
where all the fighting was going on, the Debaltseve
area. And of course, they conquered Debaltseve

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:59.650
before the fighting began to quiet down. Mr.
Putin is afraid of Russian casualties. The

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:03.970
Russian people have shown in numerous polls
conducted by the Levada Center in Moscow that

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:07.640
they don't want Russian troops fighting in
Ukraine. Mr. Putin is lying to his people

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:13.180
about his Russian troops being in Ukraine.
And casualties are a serious matter for Mr.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.900
Putin.
Mr. Nemtsov was assassinated on Friday. Not

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:21.139
because he was a great opposition leader,
but because he was going to issue a report

00:06:21.139 --> 00:06:25.400
characterizing the Russian troops who were
going to be—who were fighting in Ukraine.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:30.300
So providing weapons to Ukraine will mean
more Russian casualties, which will increase

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:35.950
the cost on Mr. Putin as a political vulnerability.
And there's really no downside to providing

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:38.870
such weapons, because we're already accused
by the Russians not just of providing weapons

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:42.280
to Ukraine, but of having American special
forces fighting in Ukraine.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:46.760
DEYOUNG: Is that the—is that really the
only reason why it's a good idea, because

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:50.990
it will increase Russian casualties? Because
I think there's pretty widespread agreement

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:57.260
that they're not going—you know, that Ukraine
can't win against Russia. So is that the primary

00:06:57.260 --> 00:07:00.980
reason to do it?
HERBST: The point to this, the greatest challenge

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:05.130
to national security in the world today is
the revisionism of Mr. Putin and his&nbsp;(inaudible)

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:10.020
Moscow. They have ambitions that go well beyond
Ukraine. They claim the right to defend Russians

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.030
and Russian-speakers wherever they reside.
And Russian-speakers reside in great quantities

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:19.770
in two Baltic allies, Estonia and Latvia.
They also (inaudible) substantial number in

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:21.990
Kazakhstan.
Mr. Putin has conducted provocations against

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:26.680
the Baltic states, and his ambitions go beyond
Ukraine. You provide weapons to Ukraine, you

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:31.700
increase the price on Mr. Putin for his aggression.
That gives him less to fight with elsewhere,

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:36.090
wherever that elsewhere may be.
DEYOUNG: Do you—Matt, I've heard a couple

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:41.440
of speeches that you've given recently. And
you've said that—that arming Ukraine would

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.669
be courting disaster and would be pushing
Putin into a corner. Can you explain that

00:07:46.669 --> 00:07:50.860
a bit why this is not a good idea?
ROJANSKY: Sure. Thank you for the question

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:58.840
and for this opportunity. I'm not—if I've
said that, I want to go back and check. Because

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:05.030
that's not quite the way I think about it.
I do nonetheless disagree with the recommendation,

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:09.889
respectfully. And even though I find some
of the analysis to be pretty persuasive. And

00:08:09.889 --> 00:08:16.900
I definitely share Ambassador Herbst's conviction
that this is a major security challenge globally

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:19.410
and for the United States. I don't know if
I would put it as number one, but it's up

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:24.240
there. There's no doubt that it's up there.
I guess I have probably three clusters of

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.480
concerns right now. But the first thing that
I would say—and I think this is important

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:32.820
at this moment. You started us in a kind of
cross-section snapshot, where are we right

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:38.611
now. Where we are right now is that—this
is just a factual statement—this question

00:08:38.611 --> 00:08:43.150
is functionally moot for the moment.
And&nbsp;what I mean by that is the White House

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:48.110
made its disinclination to send weapons very
clear from what, two, three months ago? I

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:53.440
think we've heard that. That hasn't changed.
I think they've been very smart. I agree and

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:57.230
would support what the President and Secretary
Kerry have done in terms of this intentional

00:08:57.230 --> 00:09:01.580
ambiguity, right? Using the fact that it's
been raised—you know, unlike Mr. Putin,

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:06.010
we don't control the public discourse in America.
And once the implicit threat has been raised,

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:08.980
use it, negotiate in the shadow of it. That
was very smart.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:12.860
I think it's also moot because it's clear
that Paris and Berlin are not behind it right

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:18.770
now. And in that sense, that they also support
continuing to kind of wait and see as far

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:24.690
as Minsk is concerned. You know, look, the
problem with Minsk is that you can characterize

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:27.970
it more or less however you want, right?
If you want to characterize it as a complete

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:32.220
failure, you can do that. This is a confirmation
bias problem, right? If you go in looking

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:36.839
for an opportunity to say well, there's been
some shelling, there's been continued fighting,

00:09:36.839 --> 00:09:41.160
so we have no ceasefire. That's an accurate
statement. We have no ceasefire, right? The

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.260
shooting hadn't stopped.
On the other hand, if you want to say we have

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:48.400
progress, right? People are alive today who
wouldn't have been alive if we hadn't had

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:53.130
a Minsk process. The line has stabilized in
place where it wouldn't have done so if we

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:57.041
didn't have a Minsk process.
And I would say very importantly, we have

00:09:57.041 --> 00:10:03.330
a reaffirmation in a document, that you now
have major international players including,

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:06.720
importantly, Russia. That's not to say that
you that you take that promise to the bank.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:11.670
But at least it's a statement that endorsed
a framework for the next stage, right,for

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:18.380
what does the timetable look like for how
you get to a new shape of Ukraine in which

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:22.930
the separatist entities and the government
in Ukraine could actually coexist. And then

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:26.210
the government in Ukraine can get about its
business, which is fixing Ukraine.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:32.120
DEYOUNG: And why would that—why would that
process not be helped, rather than hindered,

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:36.510
by increasing the defensive capabilities of
the Ukrainian military?

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:41.360
ROJANSKY: Yes. I'm—I am all for increasing
the defensive capabilities of the Ukrainian

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:46.050
military. I want to be very clear about that.
And that kind of speaks to—to kind of the

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:50.930
first concern that I have out of—out of
three that I'm—that are sort of at least

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:53.980
worth raising here.
And this is one where I'm really open to being

00:10:53.980 --> 00:11:00.330
persuaded. But I'm genuinely concerned that
the proposal to send weapons, this particular

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:04.470
proposal to send these particular weapons,
we don't know how it ends in terms of what

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:08.340
happens to the weapons. Are they used effectively
by the Ukrainian military? And I know that—I

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:12.180
know that senior Ukrainian security officials
told the group that went and visited—because

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:16.520
Ambassador&nbsp;Piper had&nbsp;mentioned this to me,
this is what they want. OK. I've been working

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:20.930
with Ukrainians for a long time. They tell
us a lot of stuff about what they want. (Inaudible),

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:25.660
you know that too. OK. It doesn't necessarily
translate to this is exactly what's going

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:29.010
to make the best difference that we can right
now pound for pound.

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:33.180
But the bigger concern is what happens to
the weapons in this conflict zone? So we saw—what

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:36.710
was it, about a week, week and a half ago,
that one of the three counter-battery radar

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:40.410
systems the United States has already sent—and
by the way, there was chatter about this two

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:43.870
weeks ago already. It was confirmed. It was
front page in the New York Times—is now

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:48.430
in separatist hands. Right?
So we have a kind of concern about an Afghanistan

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:53.020
scenario, where once you put the weapons into
the conflict zone, what happens? Do you just

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:56.680
end up with a lot more people dead? Do you
end up with a lot more civilians dead?

00:11:56.680 --> 00:12:00.950
My second concern is the Russian reaction.
And I want to be very clear about this. I

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:06.310
don't mean I'm worried we provoke Mr. Putin
and he gets madder. Right? Mr. Putin's as

00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:12.460
mad as he's going to be, right? He is a cold,
calculating, very difficult, nasty adversary.

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:16.570
I would say let's presume that he's going
to escalate. Let's presume that he's going

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:21.709
to behave absolutely as badly as he can.
Then the question you have to ask is OK, if

00:12:21.709 --> 00:12:25.650
Russia is prepared to go to the nth degree
and to escalate against Ukraine and against

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:29.620
what it will then perceive as, in fact, you
know, explicitly a Western-backed Ukraine—though

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:33.600
I agree with Ambassador Herbst—that argument
is already made—and the Ukrainians lose

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:37.649
anyway. Do we want to have been called out?
Do we want to have been in the position where

00:12:37.649 --> 00:12:41.040
we threaten hey, we're going to send weapons.
Of course, we're not going to send boots on

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:45.920
the ground. We're not actually going to intervene
to stop you. But we've been called out. Our

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:48.130
credibility's on the line, and the Russians
still win.

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:52.540
And then the last—the last concern I have—and
this is—this is sort of related, is to what

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:57.010
degree is the debate about this one particular
policy step a substitute for the conversation

00:12:57.010 --> 00:13:01.149
we began to have earlier? Which is, what in
fact, does the West really need to do about

00:13:01.149 --> 00:13:04.930
Ukraine? What is the strategic vision for
Ukraine? What's the conversation about Ukraine?

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:08.970
I'm concerned that there's a false choice
being set up here. If you support Ukraine,

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:13.760
you're going to send a bunch of crates full
of missiles. And if you don't do that, then

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:18.190
you're appeasing Putin the dictator.
Turns out, there are some other options. And

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:20.580
some of them are pretty decent options. And
I hope we can talk about those.

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:26.910
DEYOUNG: Well, I'd like to ask both just to
sort of go back and as scholars and people

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:32.180
who know a lot about this part of the world
over a long period of time to look back just

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:40.390
briefly on how we got here and—and whether
we truly understand what Russia is up to and—and

00:13:40.390 --> 00:13:48.300
what—what role Ukraine plays in that.
Sometimes I think that with the sanctions

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:53.670
and everything, we—we sort of project what
we think would happen if similar actions were

00:13:53.670 --> 00:14:03.560
taken against this country; that if the economy
tanked really badly in a way that was related

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:09.580
to an aspect of foreign policy, that people
here wouldn't stand for a government that

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:13.130
did that.
Are we—are we just projecting onto a system

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:18.420
in Russia that really doesn't work that way?
And at the same time, we're accusing Putin

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:24.010
of really being embarked on the first or second
or third step, depending on how you look at

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:30.950
the past ten years, say, of expanding Russian
influence in the region into the Balkans,

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:36.829
into—into the Baltic countries, and into
other eastern Europe places.

00:14:36.829 --> 00:14:40.430
How much truth is there to either of those
things, in terms of what the reaction's likely

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:45.820
to be in Russia and what Putin thinks he's
doing? Whichever of you would like to talk

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:47.279
about that.
(UNKNOWN): Please.

00:14:47.279 --> 00:14:53.240
HERBST: I will, if you don't mind, answer
your question. But also, answer something

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:57.209
that Matt said.
Yes, I'm associated with a group that argues

00:14:57.209 --> 00:15:04.380
for the supply of weapons, defensive weapons,
to Ukraine. Not missiles, by the way. And

00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:07.670
I think it's part—but that's only part of
the broader strategy that I would recommend.

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:12.000
And that's a strategy based upon the challenge
we're facing.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:17.089
And again, I think the fact that the world—one
of the world's two largest nuclear powers

00:15:17.089 --> 00:15:20.940
with one of the world's largest armies and
the sixth or seventh or eighth largest economy

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:28.450
in the world has dismantled borders in two
European countries within the last six years,

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:32.620
suggests we have a serious national security
problem. I say easily the most serious compared

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:36.990
to ISIL, which is getting all the attention,
compared to Iran, even compared to North Korea.

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:42.269
And if I think if we understand that, we need
to understand that the danger we're facing

00:15:42.269 --> 00:15:47.279
requires a serious multifaceted response.
The weapons to Ukraine is just one aspect

00:15:47.279 --> 00:15:50.740
of it.
The second point that you make, which is on

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:57.370
sanctions. Sanctions will seriously hinder,
seriously weaken the Russian economy, especially

00:15:57.370 --> 00:16:04.770
given the drop in oil prices. A third element
that we need is a NATO policy which recognizes

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:10.600
the danger. What we did at Wales was highly
inadequate to the danger we face. There should

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:15.940
be a review of the U.S.-Russia relationship
in NATO.&nbsp;Excuse me,&nbsp;the Russia-NATO relationship,

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:20.399
the doctrine. There should be much more serious
deployment of Western forces to the Baltic

00:16:20.399 --> 00:16:26.681
States, which are vulnerable if, in fact,
the Russians wanted to move. And again, we

00:16:26.681 --> 00:16:30.890
should be providing arms to Ukraine.
And finally, the fourth element of our strategy

00:16:30.890 --> 00:16:36.350
should be a wrapping up of radio free Europe,
radio liberty, all the information apparatus

00:16:36.350 --> 00:16:42.180
of the West. Because Mr. Putin is running
the most comprehensive propaganda campaign

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:47.519
in world's history. And he is an admitted
admirer of Joseph Goebbels.

00:16:47.519 --> 00:16:51.179
Now, you ask are we simply mirror-imaging
as we look at what we should be doing with

00:16:51.179 --> 00:16:58.120
Mr. Putin. And the answer is no. We know this:
We know that Mr. Putin made a quote, unquote,

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:01.660
implicit deal with the Russian people, I give
you prosperity, you give me the right to do

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:07.279
whatever I want in power. And he's not delivering
on prosperity. And the sanctions make it harder

00:17:07.279 --> 00:17:09.970
for him to deliver on prosperity. That's point
one.

00:17:09.970 --> 00:17:14.389
Point two, again I already mentioned that
his—the Russian people do not want to be

00:17:14.389 --> 00:17:22.209
sending soldiers to fight&nbsp;fellow Slavs&nbsp;in
Ukraine. So providing Ukrainians a way to

00:17:22.209 --> 00:17:26.369
create more casualties is a serious impediment
to Mr. Putin's aggression.

00:17:26.369 --> 00:17:32.110
And—and finally, if you understand that
Mr. Putin's ambitions go beyond Ukraine, even

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:37.269
the fact that he could escalate in Ukraine
and quote, unquote, win, he'll pay higher

00:17:37.269 --> 00:17:41.600
price for that in terms of soldiers lost,
in terms of equipment lost. He'll pay a high

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:45.580
price forthat around terms of strengthening
the spine of Europe to maintain and strengthen

00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:48.260
sanctions.
We need to understand that the problem we're

00:17:48.260 --> 00:17:53.289
facing is not a Ukraine problem, it's a Putin
problem. And the way we beat him is in Ukraine.

00:17:53.289 --> 00:17:56.059
Thank you.
DEYOUNG: You want to --

00:17:56.059 --> 00:18:01.049
ROJANSKY: Sure, yes. So—so just on the last
one, I think about it a little bit differently.

00:18:01.049 --> 00:18:07.460
I'm actually very glad to—to have the door
open to kind of the broader set of policy

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:10.399
responses, because I think it gives us an
opportunity to talk about OK, so why, in fact,

00:18:10.399 --> 00:18:15.049
does Ukraine matter, right? Why should we
bother having a longterm interest in Ukraine?

00:18:15.049 --> 00:18:19.489
You know, is Ukraine a part of Europe that
we want to be committed to? And why does Europe

00:18:19.489 --> 00:18:22.429
matter to the United States?
The sort of questions that, you know, you

00:18:22.429 --> 00:18:27.100
ask when you're having a serious policy conversation.
And when you're contemplating, by the way,

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:30.850
committing the kind of resources that I think
we would both agree actually will be needed.

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:34.679
And here we're talking about the sort of tens
of billions scale of resources.

00:18:34.679 --> 00:18:39.489
You know, let's not forget how much money
the West put into the former Warsaw Pact countries

00:18:39.489 --> 00:18:43.989
and the Baltic States coming out of 1989 to
1991 to get the result that we can all be

00:18:43.989 --> 00:18:47.529
pretty satisfied with today, which is that
we have more or less, plus or minus, have,

00:18:47.529 --> 00:18:54.669
you know, a pretty functional, big Europe.
The default glide path towards that has broken

00:18:54.669 --> 00:18:58.279
down. We can't do the same thing again and
expect the same result.

00:18:58.279 --> 00:19:04.690
In terms of the Russian side of that equation,
sanctions have absolutely worked, but depends

00:19:04.690 --> 00:19:09.779
on how you define "worked", right? So sanctions
can do three things. One is they can send

00:19:09.779 --> 00:19:13.669
a message. They have sent a message. They
have sent a very strong message. The Russian

00:19:13.669 --> 00:19:17.679
economy is hurting. It is not exclusively
because of sanctions. It's not even exclusively

00:19:17.679 --> 00:19:21.759
because of sanctions and the oil price. It's
because sanctions, the oil price, and ridiculous

00:19:21.759 --> 00:19:25.480
structural dysfunction within the Russia economy
that any of us who've worked in Russia for

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:29.919
the last&nbsp;twenty-five years have known about
and seen, in fact, getting worse.

00:19:29.919 --> 00:19:32.799
That message has been sent. I think a message
has been sent, by the way, to countries other

00:19:32.799 --> 00:19:36.529
than Russia that might contemplate 'do we
want to change borders by force, do we want

00:19:36.529 --> 00:19:40.340
to take this little piece of territory, do
we want to muck around in our neighbors?'

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:43.264
It's not to say that we'll stop them, right?
And this is the problem we have, too; we have

00:19:43.264 --> 00:19:47.669
to think about, you know, this Putin problem,
as Ambassador Tefft defines it—excuse me.

00:19:47.669 --> 00:19:51.169
Ambassador Herbst. That's a compliment, by
the way. I think very highly of --

00:19:51.169 --> 00:19:52.929
(CROSSTALK)
HERBST: ... put that on John. He's got (inaudible).

00:19:52.929 --> 00:19:58.980
ROJANSKY: I think very highly of both; that,
in fact, you know, there is the possibly when

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:02.970
your—when you're&nbsp;interlocutor or your adversary
simply isn't deterred.

00:20:02.970 --> 00:20:07.149
And that's sort of point number two. Do we
change Putin's behavior through sanctions?

00:20:07.149 --> 00:20:11.090
The answer so far has been no, right? It's
hard to foresee a sanctions scenario that

00:20:11.090 --> 00:20:15.440
by itself transforms Russia's behavior in
Ukraine. For a whole host of reasons. But

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:19.580
I would just leave you with one, which is
that this has become existential for within

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:24.830
Russian domestic politics. In other words,
if Putin loses in Ukraine, it's pretty clear

00:20:24.830 --> 00:20:29.039
that that's the beginning of the unraveling
of Putin's power and of the Putin system in

00:20:29.039 --> 00:20:31.899
Russia.
It's much more than just the calculus 'I keep

00:20:31.899 --> 00:20:35.830
you rich, fat, and happy and, you know, you
vote for me'. It's also the idea that he is

00:20:35.830 --> 00:20:40.489
a czar, right? He is infallible. His vision
for what was going on in Ukraine was always

00:20:40.489 --> 00:20:44.450
right, remains right, his predictions for
what happens next and his interventions are&nbsp;all

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:49.149
right. And if we prove that to have been wrong,
that will be the beginning of the end. But

00:20:49.149 --> 00:20:52.830
the Ukrainians bear the biggest burden there.
And then I think the third piece of it is

00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:58.830
what, in fact, happens in that comes to pass.
So sanctions, what happens in Ukraine,a lot

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:03.090
of other factors can affect whether the Putin
regime is long for this Earth.

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:06.509
If it's not, then we have to start asking
some questions that are going to completely

00:21:06.509 --> 00:21:11.310
transform this strategic conversation in Washington.
What do we think about weak Russia? What do

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:16.440
we think about a disfunctional Russia? What
do we think about a Russia that's breaking

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:23.899
up into multiple Russias? An intensely nationalist
Russia? A caliphate in the north Caucusus?

00:21:23.899 --> 00:21:28.789
An oil-rich, population-poor Siberian Russia?
These kinds of scenarios become actually quite

00:21:28.789 --> 00:21:33.269
real and quite scary when we look at what's
happening to the Russian economy and the impact

00:21:33.269 --> 00:21:36.919
that this Western punishment that we're talking
about, in fact, can have. You put all of those

00:21:36.919 --> 00:21:39.289
together.
And then I would just say one last thing,

00:21:39.289 --> 00:21:48.789
which is that if you look at—at what happened
this weekend, this really tragic murder of

00:21:48.789 --> 00:21:54.450
Boris Nemstov. And let me also say, I mean,
I'm still in shock from that; right? I mean,

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:58.889
this—the man was the definition of, like,
life; right? He was very alive. Huge personality.

00:21:58.889 --> 00:22:03.590
Whatever you thought about his politics or
his methods, to see him made into a lifeless

00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:08.730
corpse stood up—you know, lying down but
pushed up right in front of the view of the

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:13.489
Kremlin, it's—it turns your stomach, and
it tells you that Russia has changed. Russia

00:22:13.489 --> 00:22:19.659
is different today—Sunday morning than it
was on Saturday.

00:22:19.659 --> 00:22:26.149
And so my message here is, Putin has, in fact,
opened a can of worms with Ukraine, with the

00:22:26.149 --> 00:22:31.499
rise of this angry Russian nationalism which
is absolutely committed to a victory in Ukraine

00:22:31.499 --> 00:22:36.009
at all costs that is probably going to be
a bigger problem for him and the stability

00:22:36.009 --> 00:22:39.840
of Russia, to be totally frank, than it is
at the end of the day for the West. But that

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:42.869
also becomes our problem. That is huge, huge
problem.

00:22:42.869 --> 00:22:47.729
DEYOUNG: Well, I think this gets to some of
the things that you were talking about, Ambassador.

00:22:47.729 --> 00:22:57.590
Is it—is it—because of some of the possible
problems that—that were just outlined, is

00:22:57.590 --> 00:23:06.299
it OK for the West to at this point allow
Russian—Russia to have a substantive influence

00:23:06.299 --> 00:23:14.269
in Eastern Ukraine? I mean, for a true federalist
system in which that part of the country is

00:23:14.269 --> 00:23:19.239
under Russia's sway. Is it—or has it gone
past that point? Do we need to put him more

00:23:19.239 --> 00:23:24.590
in his place and not allow that to happen?
HERBST: OK. I think there's some implicit

00:23:24.590 --> 00:23:30.730
understanding in your question, which are
in my judgment not quite on target.

00:23:30.730 --> 00:23:37.619
What you have in Ukraine is a Russian, again,
led, financed, partly staffed, and certainly

00:23:37.619 --> 00:23:43.321
equipped war against Ukraine. And the reason
why you have that is because the people of

00:23:43.321 --> 00:23:47.611
the East did not rise up against Kiev. So
what you're really asking is, can the West

00:23:47.611 --> 00:23:53.249
live with Mr. Putin essentially having control
because he established—he ran this hybrid

00:23:53.249 --> 00:23:57.149
war in Ukraine's East?
The answer to that is yes, but there's one

00:23:57.149 --> 00:24:03.409
person who can't accept that, and that's Mr.
Putin because that's not his objective. His

00:24:03.409 --> 00:24:07.760
objective in Ukraine is this: his preferred
objective, he cannot achieve, which is to

00:24:07.760 --> 00:24:11.789
have a government in Kiev which is compliant
to his wishes. And he cannot achieve it because

00:24:11.789 --> 00:24:16.279
a substantial percentage, 80-90 percent, of
the Ukrainian people despise him intensely.

00:24:16.279 --> 00:24:22.419
And I'm speaking diplomatically when I use
that formulation.

00:24:22.419 --> 00:24:28.700
So his lesser objective, which is perfectly
acceptable to him, is to make sure Ukraine

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:36.479
is destabilized. What he cannot accept, given
his outlook, is a Ukraine, even a truncated

00:24:36.479 --> 00:24:42.029
Ukraine, which is stable, democratic, prosperous,
and looking westward.

00:24:42.029 --> 00:24:47.139
That's why—you know, this is now March.
You've had this crisis in Ukraine's east for

00:24:47.139 --> 00:24:54.210
over&nbsp;eleven months. That's why, despite efforts
from every statesman west of Moscow to reach

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:59.129
out to Mr. Putin, we have never heard what
he needs to stop the fighting.&nbsp;Because what

00:24:59.129 --> 00:25:04.190
he needs is not a specific territorial objective.
It's this objective of did he stabilize Ukraine.

00:25:04.190 --> 00:25:07.710
And obviously, that's not something you can
say in polite circles.

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:12.600
So, I mean, I've said—I've been on record
of saying that if I was in Ukraine, I'd seek

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:17.480
to cauterize the East. Give Mr. Putin east.
In fact, I'd say you annex it. You paid for

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:21.149
it, you broke it. But of course, he doesn't
want that. He can't accept that. He needs

00:25:21.149 --> 00:25:26.279
to continually move beyond the borders he
controls in Ukraine's east to destabilize

00:25:26.279 --> 00:25:29.629
the country.
And people don't seem to understand this here.

00:25:29.629 --> 00:25:33.360
And again, his objectives go beyond Ukraine.
They go throughout the entire post-Soviet

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:39.619
space and maybe else. He is the number one
menace to global security today. And until

00:25:39.619 --> 00:25:45.659
it's understood in Berlin, in Washington,
London, Paris, we're not going to have the

00:25:45.659 --> 00:25:48.820
right policies.
DEYOUNG: I have lots of questions. But did

00:25:48.820 --> 00:25:52.779
you—did you want to respond, specifically?
ROJANSKY: No. I mean, look, I think—again,

00:25:52.779 --> 00:25:56.360
I agree. We—we have a—we have a Putin
problem. We also have a Russia problem. And

00:25:56.360 --> 00:26:00.720
we also have a Ukraine problem. The problem
is we have all of those problems.

00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:06.370
And—and what that tends to suggest is—you
know, again, foreign policy's hard. That's

00:26:06.370 --> 00:26:10.149
why we have these kinds of conversations.
We've got to figure out, you know, what are

00:26:10.149 --> 00:26:12.429
your priorities? And then you've got to&nbsp;talk
about timing.

00:26:12.429 --> 00:26:18.539
So my sense is there probably is a way or
less to bring the fighting to an end. And

00:26:18.539 --> 00:26:25.940
I am reasonably—I'm not satisfied. That's
not the right word. But I'm relieved at the

00:26:25.940 --> 00:26:31.809
degree to which Minsk actually has delivered
a reduction in violence. It is not complete.

00:26:31.809 --> 00:26:36.649
We need to see more. But that gets me thinking
in terms of timing and sequence.

00:26:36.649 --> 00:26:40.679
And I actually think there's a way. Because
the beauty of the situation—maybe the irony

00:26:40.679 --> 00:26:46.110
of it—is I don't think—I don't think that
Putin has another&nbsp;ten years or&nbsp;twenty years.

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:50.769
I think Ukrainian project is a&nbsp;ten year or&nbsp;twenty
year project, just as the European project

00:26:50.769 --> 00:26:56.639
was a half century project.
And I think if we can—if we can get an end

00:26:56.639 --> 00:27:02.009
to the fighting in the immediate short term,
if we can transform this urgency—you know,

00:27:02.009 --> 00:27:06.169
Ambassador Herbst mentioned polling numbers,
right? So yes, you're right. A huge number

00:27:06.169 --> 00:27:12.059
of people hate Putin. A disturbing number
of people even now don't like Russians. Big

00:27:12.059 --> 00:27:15.419
problem, right, when you have—your biggest
neighbor.

00:27:15.419 --> 00:27:19.580
But more important than that, an overwhelming
majority of people are committed now to this

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:24.649
vision of European integration. There's even
a pretty large preponderance that wants to

00:27:24.649 --> 00:27:29.039
be in NATO. That is a transformation of the
Ukrainian domestic landscape. That creates

00:27:29.039 --> 00:27:35.070
an opportunity to actually fulfill a reform
vision in Ukraine that has never happened

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:38.299
in&nbsp;twenty-five years.
Because the thing about the EU and NATO, if

00:27:38.299 --> 00:27:42.730
you take them seriously, is that they are
reform agendas. That's what they are, right?

00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:47.169
Yes, there's Article 5. Yes, there's economic
benefit that comes with the EU. But in essence,

00:27:47.169 --> 00:27:52.549
they are roadmaps to reform. They are how
to become a modern Western European, you know,

00:27:52.549 --> 00:27:56.929
or Norther American-type country.
If Ukraine, in the sort of middle term of

00:27:56.929 --> 00:28:02.029
this strategy, can transform that anger, the
urgency, everything that comes out of the

00:28:02.029 --> 00:28:05.289
war and the feeling of frustration that they
have now into the kind of resolve they've

00:28:05.289 --> 00:28:09.730
never had before. Never, ever. And I mean
on the elite level and the level of society

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:13.929
to fulfill the reform agenda, that will be
a victory, no matter what Putin continues

00:28:13.929 --> 00:28:18.039
to do. No matter how much trouble he keeps
stirring up, that's the victory scenario in

00:28:18.039 --> 00:28:20.419
the middle term.
In the longer term—and here I have to say

00:28:20.419 --> 00:28:25.460
something that's difficult, but it's reality.
The longer term is you need to have a vision

00:28:25.460 --> 00:28:29.999
for reconciliation and reintegration within
Ukraine and between Ukraine and Russia. And

00:28:29.999 --> 00:28:34.220
if you don't have that, if you say "oh, well,
how can we talk about that because we're at

00:28:34.220 --> 00:28:38.430
war"? Then you condemn yourself to a vision
of Ukraine that will be a failure, I promise

00:28:38.430 --> 00:28:43.080
you that. Because Ukraine isn't Poland. It
is not 99 percent Polish and Catholic. And

00:28:43.080 --> 00:28:48.109
it never will be.
DEYOUNG: Let's go to questions from the floor.

00:28:48.109 --> 00:28:53.379
If you could, I'll point to you. And then
if you could wait for the microphone and identify

00:28:53.379 --> 00:29:02.469
yourself and your affiliation. Yes, sir.
QUESTION: Thank you. Bill Courtney with Rand.

00:29:02.469 --> 00:29:09.080
Very good presentation.
Mariupol is roughly&nbsp;twenty times larger than

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:16.700
Debaltseve. The Russians had a harder time
taking Debaltseve and also the Donetsk airport

00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:21.539
than they thought. The Ukrainian military
resistance at first was feckless. Now, they

00:29:21.539 --> 00:29:25.879
appear to have learned something about fighting
in urban-like environments.

00:29:25.879 --> 00:29:34.330
If Putin's goal is (inaudible) or even a land
bridge to Crimea, Mariupol has to be&nbsp;the

00:29:34.330 --> 00:29:40.470
next strategic target. Do you think Putin
would be willing to take the risk of taking

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:50.019
on a city that large?
ROJANSKY: Please. I'm --

00:29:50.019 --> 00:29:57.210
HERBST: I don't think there's any question.
It's probably implicitly evident in what I've

00:29:57.210 --> 00:30:00.529
already said; that Mr. Putin is going to strike
elsewhere in Ukraine because he can't let

00:30:00.529 --> 00:30:05.809
things stabilize. He cannot have a true frozen
conflict, because his objective is to destabilize

00:30:05.809 --> 00:30:10.700
the country. Mariupol seems to be the next
objective.

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:17.029
And by the way, I can in no way share Matt's
optimism about the ceasefire. Because we had—when

00:30:17.029 --> 00:30:22.299
there have been quiet periods in the ceasefire—remember,
we had&nbsp;in Donetsk&nbsp;one ceasefire from early

00:30:22.299 --> 00:30:26.559
September. The quiet periods have been used
by the Kremlin to send substantial assessments

00:30:26.559 --> 00:30:32.549
of advanced equipment.
The most quiet period was between, like, December

00:30:32.549 --> 00:30:38.600
9 and January 9. And that's when the Kremlin
sent in hundreds of missiles, tanks, armored

00:30:38.600 --> 00:30:43.090
personnel carriers. Because their objective
is to go further into Ukraine to destabilize

00:30:43.090 --> 00:30:45.950
the country.
Bill's right that the Ukrainian forces have

00:30:45.950 --> 00:30:50.840
fought pretty well. Russia's lost a lot of
people. And the separatists have quote, unquote,

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:55.779
lost a lot of people at Donetsk Airport and
the Debaltseve. If they try to take Mariupol,

00:30:55.779 --> 00:31:01.779
they'll lose even more.
But there will problems elsewhere. And Mariupol

00:31:01.779 --> 00:31:05.350
seems to be the most likely, although not
the only place where they might strike.

00:31:05.350 --> 00:31:09.999
DEYOUNG: Matt, did you want to --
ROJANSKY: Yes. I mean, my only—my only thought

00:31:09.999 --> 00:31:18.039
on Mariupol specifically was that it was about
two weeks ago this conversation was in full

00:31:18.039 --> 00:31:23.099
swing about, you know, is Mariupol the next
Debaltseve. And, you know, I had a conversation

00:31:23.099 --> 00:31:28.109
with a Ukrainian friend where he just basically
pointed at the map. And he said, you know,

00:31:28.109 --> 00:31:33.119
this land bridge thing is a fantasy. Maybe
it wasn't a year ago. Maybe Novorossiya a

00:31:33.119 --> 00:31:37.599
year ago seemed like it could happen and it
would be this sort of boomerang-shaped thing

00:31:37.599 --> 00:31:41.119
that would go all the way to Crimea and even
to Odessa and even to Transnistria, right?

00:31:41.119 --> 00:31:43.239
This is a complete fantasy. It's not going
to happen.

00:31:43.239 --> 00:31:48.080
The reason it's not going to happen though
is not because the Russian military couldn't

00:31:48.080 --> 00:31:54.719
if they wanted to grind into dust everything
between the border and Odessa, right? I mean,

00:31:54.719 --> 00:31:59.499
they have the hardware to do it. And if they're
ordered, they'll do it and it will be a horrible

00:31:59.499 --> 00:32:05.389
catastrophe. But it won't end, right? It will
be an endless, insurgent-type war of the kind

00:32:05.389 --> 00:32:09.589
that Ukraine has actually seen through a number
of different phases of its history. And it

00:32:09.589 --> 00:32:13.580
would be a total nightmare.
Now, is it utterly—can I guarantee you right

00:32:13.580 --> 00:32:18.820
now Mr. Putin will not do that? No, I can't.
It is within the realm of possibly. It seems

00:32:18.820 --> 00:32:23.399
to me that if the conversations about Mariupol—and
this is the one thing that I find a little

00:32:23.399 --> 00:32:29.090
irritating about the kind of Mariupol-is-next,
Mariupol-is-next mantra, is that that would

00:32:29.090 --> 00:32:36.239
seem to me to be very unlikely. It doesn't
track with the path so far.

00:32:36.239 --> 00:32:40.760
I do think—and if I could just comment on—on
this phrase that Ambassador Herbst used about,

00:32:40.760 --> 00:32:44.960
you know Putin cannot let things stabilize.
Putin has to open a new front. He has to attack

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:51.440
somewhere. You know, that might be right.
The problem with that, if that's the reality

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:55.960
that we're dealing with, then there is only
one solution to this, and that's regime change

00:32:55.960 --> 00:32:59.529
in Russia.
And that's an exceptionally fraught and dangerous

00:32:59.529 --> 00:33:05.049
solution for the West. That's where I say
we have to have a much bigger conversation.

00:33:05.049 --> 00:33:08.309
And I'm prepared to have that conversation.
But I'm not prepared to put the cart before

00:33:08.309 --> 00:33:13.499
the horse and commit to policies that lead
to that without knowing where we're going.

00:33:13.499 --> 00:33:18.819
DEYOUNG: Hand up here. Yes.
QUESTION: One thing that's different in the

00:33:18.819 --> 00:33:19.859
--
DEYOUNG:&nbsp;Can you&nbsp;identify yourself?

00:33:19.859 --> 00:33:23.679
THE DEFENDANT: I'm sorry.&nbsp;Anton (inaudible)
from&nbsp;(inaudible).

00:33:23.679 --> 00:33:27.429
One thing that's different in the toolkit
of American foreign policy ten years ago and

00:33:27.429 --> 00:33:31.759
today is that the U.S. is not only becoming
energy independent, but it can export. And

00:33:31.759 --> 00:33:38.590
it can export lots of gas, et cetera.
What signal would it give to Putin—just

00:33:38.590 --> 00:33:44.469
fantasize here for a moment—if the United
States made it clear that it would be prepared

00:33:44.469 --> 00:33:52.049
to do the (inaudible) the Berlin airlift and
say if you run short of gas, we'll provide

00:33:52.049 --> 00:33:58.229
it no questions asked. Money, we'll discuss
later, but it will be there. Which it could

00:33:58.229 --> 00:34:06.499
do.
HERBST: A very interesting question. I'm not—I'm

00:34:06.499 --> 00:34:12.080
not an expert on hydrocarbons. But I'm not
certain the United States, even in its new

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:18.360
hydrocarbon wealth, has the ability to supply
all of Europe's needs with either liquified

00:34:18.360 --> 00:34:23.360
natural gas or anything else.
Having said that, I concede your point. And

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:28.890
I agree, if we were to open up our energy
for export—which we'd have to do by law—that

00:34:28.890 --> 00:34:33.669
would be a strong signal not just of support
for the Europeans&nbsp;vis-a-vis dealing with

00:34:33.669 --> 00:34:39.820
the problem of Russian control over gas supplies
to Europe. It would also probably bring a

00:34:39.820 --> 00:34:44.580
drop in natural gas prices over the world.
So I strongly support it.

00:34:44.580 --> 00:34:55.280
DEYOUNG: Yes sir.
QUESTION: Bob Blake. Nobody's mentioned Germany

00:34:55.280 --> 00:35:06.360
yet. And for our prime minister, where—it
seems to me that that's a key element there.

00:35:06.360 --> 00:35:16.040
Where are things now, not only with—with
her and with her party, but with the German

00:35:16.040 --> 00:35:22.790
business community? What—what kind of—of
limits does that make on our own policy?

00:35:22.790 --> 00:35:26.520
DEYOUNG: I think—and and I would like to
expand that question just a little bit.

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:34.150
You mentioned NATO, Ambassador. It would seem
to me that NATO is pretty far from having

00:35:34.150 --> 00:35:41.120
any kind of consensus about—about what to
do about expanding its activities in its eastern

00:35:41.120 --> 00:35:51.750
members. And I think that the same disagreement
exists on—on some of these economic questions

00:35:51.750 --> 00:35:56.720
as it does on—on the—on the—the military
questions.

00:35:56.720 --> 00:36:01.960
So I wonder if either or both of you could
address that with Germany and the larger question

00:36:01.960 --> 00:36:07.630
of Europe.
ROJANSKY: Sure. Yes. OK. So on Germany, I

00:36:07.630 --> 00:36:13.830
can't speak as a Germanist at all. But I will
say that gosh, it's probably almost a year

00:36:13.830 --> 00:36:18.650
ago at this point that I wrote a piece—I
think it was for CNN—in which one of the

00:36:18.650 --> 00:36:23.430
main points I made&nbsp;about how you actually
deal with the security dimension and strategic

00:36:23.430 --> 00:36:28.520
dimension of this crisis boils down mostly
to kind of 'let Europe lead'. Which might

00:36:28.520 --> 00:36:33.980
sound very much like the sort of, you know,
leading from behind. You know, America should—not

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:39.170
at all, right?
The rationale on my side is I very much think

00:36:39.170 --> 00:36:44.550
of the European project, of the post-world
War&nbsp;II era and in particular, the post-Helsinki

00:36:44.550 --> 00:36:50.000
era, to be the number one achievement for
American national security in—at least in

00:36:50.000 --> 00:36:57.230
this part of the hemisphere. And for that
reason, I think kind of wagging that dog,

00:36:57.230 --> 00:37:02.340
you know, from the American tail, was always
going to weaken a project that's already under

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:05.550
threat; for its own domestic reasons. You
know, within Europe, the (inaudible) and all

00:37:05.550 --> 00:37:10.120
that business. But also for the challenge
that Mr. Putin has posed. So I am extremely

00:37:10.120 --> 00:37:14.620
pleased—extremely pleased—with Merkel's
leadership role here.

00:37:14.620 --> 00:37:20.430
Let me just say quickly three things. One,
short term, my understanding is that the German

00:37:20.430 --> 00:37:26.430
business community will absolutely remain
a check, a break, but not a brick wall on

00:37:26.430 --> 00:37:32.370
Germany moving forward with enhanced pressure
on Russia. That's probably not a bad thing.

00:37:32.370 --> 00:37:36.850
Part of what I envisioned when I thought about
European leadership in this crisis was just

00:37:36.850 --> 00:37:41.800
how much more—how much less political and
how much more sort of deliberate Germany has

00:37:41.800 --> 00:37:47.010
to be when it thinks about what&nbsp;it does vis-a-vis
Russia. That's a good thing. I like that style

00:37:47.010 --> 00:37:51.700
of decision making.
Middle term, a German colleague of mine who

00:37:51.700 --> 00:37:56.710
you all probably have heard from here in this
town, Constanze Muller, says the German—and

00:37:56.710 --> 00:38:02.120
I think she's right. She says, "the German-Russian
strategic partnership is over." Right? So

00:38:02.120 --> 00:38:05.860
I think of that as being a kind of middle-term
phenomenon. It's much than they've fallen

00:38:05.860 --> 00:38:10.900
out of love but could fall back in love tomorrow.
But it's not saying Germany and Russia will

00:38:10.900 --> 00:38:15.780
be foes for the next centuries.
But for the foreseeable sort of decades ahead,

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:18.920
Germany and Russia are not going to be holding
each other's hands in each other's respective

00:38:18.920 --> 00:38:23.750
economic development the way that they have,
quite frankly, since even the Soviet era.

00:38:23.750 --> 00:38:27.590
Remember&nbsp;Adenauer /&nbsp;Khrushchev; right? This
stuff has come to an end.

00:38:27.590 --> 00:38:33.750
In the very longterm, I nonetheless believe
that Germany and Europe more broadly will

00:38:33.750 --> 00:38:40.290
also have to play a leadership role in rethinking
what Europe looks like. What a new kind of

00:38:40.290 --> 00:38:45.120
concert of Europe means, right? If you look
back at European history, you cannot escape

00:38:45.120 --> 00:38:50.400
the conclusion that every&nbsp;fifty to 100 years,
European powers have to get together in the

00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:54.670
light of changing and shifting power and shifting
pressures and figure out what are the rules

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:58.240
of the game for Europe.
Russia has to be at that table, whoever is

00:38:58.240 --> 00:39:02.330
at the helm of Russia. Germany has to be at
that table. I hope we're at that table. But

00:39:02.330 --> 00:39:05.260
it's not going to be our table. It's going
to be Europe's table.

00:39:05.260 --> 00:39:11.200
HERBST: I would agree with everything Matt
said. But I would add a few items.

00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:19.850
Of—on Germany. When Chancellor Merkel was
here a couple weeks ago and saw the President

00:39:19.850 --> 00:39:25.380
and it was clear from what they said publicly
that while Germany would not agree and they

00:39:25.380 --> 00:39:29.922
would not support, would not send weapons
to Ukraine, they would accept an American

00:39:29.922 --> 00:39:33.630
decision to do so and it would not lead to
any serious problems between Germany and the

00:39:33.630 --> 00:39:37.380
United States. And they would work to—the
Chancellor would work to maintain unity.

00:39:37.380 --> 00:39:44.100
I've been going to Berlin since this crisis
began. And I can tell you this&nbsp;—reaffirm

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:50.680
Matt's point that the—there's been a change
in the calculations in the Chancellor's office,

00:39:50.680 --> 00:39:54.360
as well as the public. There's a much bigger
appreciation of the problem that Mr. Putin

00:39:54.360 --> 00:40:00.370
represents. Although I don't think they understand
it completely, but they've come a long way.

00:40:00.370 --> 00:40:11.910
The last point, and one area I do disagree
with Matt, which is that the proper response

00:40:11.910 --> 00:40:19.350
for dealing with this crisis absolutely requires
American leadership. Because every time in

00:40:19.350 --> 00:40:24.620
the post-Cold War—excuse me—in the post-war
world, even going back to 1945, when you've

00:40:24.620 --> 00:40:31.190
faced a serious security challenge in Europe,
it was American leadership that was required.

00:40:31.190 --> 00:40:34.410
Chancellor Merkel has been in the lead because
President—our president has given her that

00:40:34.410 --> 00:40:41.030
role. Now, if he does not understand that
we face, you know, an extraordinarily great

00:40:41.030 --> 00:40:46.280
crisis from Mr. Putin, then he doesn't have
to get involved. And therefore, he's let her

00:40:46.280 --> 00:40:52.321
lead. But given the fact it has to be a strong
security response to the Kremlin; response

00:40:52.321 --> 00:40:57.410
within NATO as I described, response as part
of a group for arming Ukraine, the United

00:40:57.410 --> 00:41:01.330
States has to be out in the lead. And without
that, you will not have the proper policies.

00:41:01.330 --> 00:41:04.450
DEYOUNG: But do you think the President doesn't
understand?

00:41:04.450 --> 00:41:08.800
HERBST: I would say the policy demonstrates
that the understanding is not as strong as

00:41:08.800 --> 00:41:13.510
it should be.
DEYOUNG: Diplomat. Yes, sir.

00:41:13.510 --> 00:41:20.450
QUESTION: Lloyd&nbsp;Hand, King&nbsp;and Spalding.
In trying to be brief, sometimes it leads

00:41:20.450 --> 00:41:27.140
to oversimplistic questions. I understand—--
first of all, I should say thank you for a

00:41:27.140 --> 00:41:33.820
very informative analysis assessment, prescriptions.
It seems to me it boils down to this central

00:41:33.820 --> 00:41:39.670
question of the risk management of providing
lethal defensive weapons and what the consequences

00:41:39.670 --> 00:41:47.850
may be. Or when you look at alternative sanctions—I
mean the leverage, it's sanctions.

00:41:47.850 --> 00:41:52.970
With the divisions you just described within
Europe, I'm reminded of a conversation I had

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:57.120
with an ambassador of one of the countries
who's intimately involved in those discussions.

00:41:57.120 --> 00:42:04.220
And what he said is, neither Europe nor the
U.S. is willing to go to war over Ukraine.

00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:11.850
If that's a fact, it seems to me that the
leverage, from what you have said, that involves

00:42:11.850 --> 00:42:18.330
less divisiveness among the parties, is increased
sanctions. Now, I heard what you said about

00:42:18.330 --> 00:42:24.530
maybe the sanctions are a third of the economic
plight. But if—if what I read about the

00:42:24.530 --> 00:42:30.080
continued—or lowered—decline in oil prices
is correct, about another&nbsp;eighteen months

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:37.610
to two years, nobody probably knows beyond
that, then it seems to me that there's less

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:45.990
risk in managing these unlikely choices, to
increase the leverage in which it seems that

00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:52.240
all the parties are in agreement.
Just taking advantage of the&nbsp;sub-minor question.

00:42:52.240 --> 00:42:57.690
Going back to initially we were talking about
Kerry saying that Lavrov was lying. Don't

00:42:57.690 --> 00:43:04.190
we have, with advanced technical means and
advanced technology, the capability of determining

00:43:04.190 --> 00:43:10.540
unquestionably the extent of Russian troops
and equipment in Ukraine and the ability to

00:43:10.540 --> 00:43:14.442
make that known so that there's no equivocation
about that? Thank you.

00:43:14.442 --> 00:43:19.090
ROJANSKY: Excellent question.
DEYOUNG: Would you like to address that.

00:43:19.090 --> 00:43:22.630
ROJANSKY: Excellent questions. Yeah. Well,
but thank you. You've got it. This is great.

00:43:22.630 --> 00:43:29.390
So number one, we have—OK. I'm not privy
to classified information, nor am I revealing

00:43:29.390 --> 00:43:34.920
any here. We have a lot more than the United
States government is publicly revealing about

00:43:34.920 --> 00:43:41.220
what is actually going on on the border, on
the ground on both sides; who has been responsible

00:43:41.220 --> 00:43:45.910
for some of the different massive civilian
tragedies and infrastructure destructions

00:43:45.910 --> 00:43:49.660
that we have seen.
Why we have been so parsimonious about it,

00:43:49.660 --> 00:43:54.310
even to the point of releasing kind of ridiculously
grainy satellite images that people in the

00:43:54.310 --> 00:43:58.780
blogosphere are making fun of because it's
so obvious that it's not at all what our capabilities

00:43:58.780 --> 00:44:02.750
can do, I don't know. I don't understand.
Because it seems to me that when you're in

00:44:02.750 --> 00:44:06.950
what is clearly an information war, a war
in which sometimes what matters more is the

00:44:06.950 --> 00:44:11.320
spin that's put on things than what actually
happened; more clarity, more transparencies

00:44:11.320 --> 00:44:14.980
is very much to our advantage.
If we have a policy that's grounded in reality.

00:44:14.980 --> 00:44:19.640
If our policy's grounded in an imaginary scenario
that isn't actually provable and based in

00:44:19.640 --> 00:44:24.220
fact, well, then, you want to conceal something.
But that's exactly the wrong message to send

00:44:24.220 --> 00:44:27.820
to the Ukrainian people and the Russian people.
Because they're already predisposed in this

00:44:27.820 --> 00:44:33.360
kind of post-Soviet cynicism and konspiratzia,
the sort of conspiracy rumormongering, et

00:44:33.360 --> 00:44:38.150
cetera, to assume that we are, you know, doing
more than we say we're doing and more so on

00:44:38.150 --> 00:44:41.790
and so forth. So I would absolutely argue
for enhanced transparency and enhanced information.

00:44:41.790 --> 00:44:48.160
You know, eyes on the ground. Very simple.
One positive note on that—and again, Ambassador

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:53.140
Herbst can accuse me of being the—the eternal
optimist here. The OSCE special monitoring

00:44:53.140 --> 00:44:58.580
mission now is about doubling down on its
staff. That's really, really good. And specifically,

00:44:58.580 --> 00:45:03.000
I saw the announcement&nbsp;because a friend&nbsp;who
happens to be in the personnel department

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:07.580
there sent it to me. They are specifically
looking for people, at least from the U.S.

00:45:07.580 --> 00:45:12.770
complement, with national/technical expertise,
who know how to deal with satellite intel

00:45:12.770 --> 00:45:16.720
issues. That's an extremely positive step.
It's something I've been calling for for nine

00:45:16.720 --> 00:45:19.420
months or so.
And just very quickly on your question about

00:45:19.420 --> 00:45:24.150
the spectrum of the threat, this has been
one of my concerns all along. Which is, you

00:45:24.150 --> 00:45:29.940
know, let's assume, as my colleague&nbsp;Andrey
Illarionov&nbsp;likes to say, Putin is, you know,

00:45:29.940 --> 00:45:34.740
a mugger, a bully in a dark ally, right? If
you're going to up against someone like that

00:45:34.740 --> 00:45:40.120
and your response is going to be a threat
back down or else, it shouldn't be a threat

00:45:40.120 --> 00:45:45.520
that says 'back down or else defined as between
this and this, but nothing more than that'.

00:45:45.520 --> 00:45:49.780
And that sort of is my concern with the whole
discussion. We will only ever get approval

00:45:49.780 --> 00:45:53.300
to send weapons—I want to be very clear
about this. We'll only ever get approval to

00:45:53.300 --> 00:45:58.311
send weapons if there is an ironclad guarantee
about what the ceiling is on that; that it

00:45:58.311 --> 00:46:02.240
doesn't lead to a mission creep that ends
up with American boots on the ground. We know

00:46:02.240 --> 00:46:06.990
that. So why threaten something that you know
is inherently limited when you're going against

00:46:06.990 --> 00:46:11.630
an adversary who sees this as a survival issue?
It seems to me it just won't work.

00:46:11.630 --> 00:46:20.270
HERBST: Great question, great answer. But
let me offer some additional, not necessarily

00:46:20.270 --> 00:46:25.000
completely consistent, observations.
First of all, I agree with you that sanctions

00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:29.510
are a good tool. But that doesn't mean that
providing weapons is not a good tool. And,

00:46:29.510 --> 00:46:34.700
in fact, we have created mischief for people
who needed mischief created around the world,

00:46:34.700 --> 00:46:40.480
for example, during the Reagan years with
the Reagan doctrine. We provided arms to folks

00:46:40.480 --> 00:46:50.790
fighting Communist allies, whether it was
in Angola, Afghanistan, and elsewhere, Namibia,

00:46:50.790 --> 00:46:56.200
Tanzania, to some real effect.
And the fact that Mr. Putin is willing to

00:46:56.200 --> 00:47:00.270
spend more of his&nbsp;treasury on it doesn't
mean that these methods are not going to take

00:47:00.270 --> 00:47:03.850
a toll. Which is what we want to do. We want
the aggressor to pay a price. That's point

00:47:03.850 --> 00:47:08.350
one.
Point two on intel, Matt's absolutely right.

00:47:08.350 --> 00:47:11.990
We should be providing more information. Just
like the White House should not be talking

00:47:11.990 --> 00:47:15.060
about the Russian incursion into Ukraine;
it should be taking about the Russian invasion

00:47:15.060 --> 00:47:20.740
of Ukraine. I don't know why we're not doing
it. I do know this, however: If we accurately

00:47:20.740 --> 00:47:26.000
describe what the Kremlin is up to, the pressure
on the President to take more effective action

00:47:26.000 --> 00:47:32.060
will grow. And he clearly does not want that
to grow. That's point two.

00:47:32.060 --> 00:47:36.530
Point three, Matt's part—Matt's observation,
with which I agree, that it's foolish to say

00:47:36.530 --> 00:47:39.650
you're giving weapons if at the same time
you're saying you're giving X amount of weapons

00:47:39.650 --> 00:47:44.380
and you'll never give more. That would be
a mistake.&nbsp;And I tend to agree with Matt

00:47:44.380 --> 00:47:48.120
when he says that's the only way we're going
to do it. And the only reason why that's true

00:47:48.120 --> 00:47:52.190
is because there's a failure of leadership
in Washington. If Washington understood the

00:47:52.190 --> 00:47:56.440
gravity of this crisis, it would maintain
that ambiguity, even as it sends weapons into

00:47:56.440 --> 00:48:00.370
Ukraine.
My argument is that we need to have more serious

00:48:00.370 --> 00:48:04.630
leadership on this issue in Washington. And
that's lacking. That's unfortunate. Because

00:48:04.630 --> 00:48:09.460
again, this is the signature foreign policy
crisis of this administration. And while they've

00:48:09.460 --> 00:48:13.840
done well on sanctions, they've done very
poorly on the larger security geopolitical

00:48:13.840 --> 00:48:18.250
aspect of this; whether in terms of helping
Ukraine fight aggression or getting&nbsp;NATO

00:48:18.250 --> 00:48:21.970
prepared for possible Russian games beyond
Ukraine.

00:48:21.970 --> 00:48:28.150
DEYOUNG: Yes.
QUESTION:&nbsp;Anya Schmemann,&nbsp;American University.

00:48:28.150 --> 00:48:33.110
So most conversations about Ukraine turn into
conversations about Russia, which is understandable.

00:48:33.110 --> 00:48:38.590
But let's focus for just a minute on our sick
patient in front of, Ukraine.

00:48:38.590 --> 00:48:45.600
Even if there is a successful ceasefire, Ukraine
has significant challenges. What does Ukraine

00:48:45.600 --> 00:48:51.860
need to do to solve its economic problems,
its political problems, its military problems?

00:48:51.860 --> 00:49:02.180
And how do you assess Poroshenko's own future?
ROJANSKY: Sure. Thank you, Anya. So I have

00:49:02.180 --> 00:49:08.500
been working on this Ukraine cluster of problems
for a while. I was what, sadly, I think, will

00:49:08.500 --> 00:49:13.780
be the last Title 8, that is U.S. Federal
government-funded fellow to work for Ambassador

00:49:13.780 --> 00:49:17.050
Tefft in Kiev before he left and then went
to Moscow.

00:49:17.050 --> 00:49:20.520
And I worked on this problem called "corporate
raiding." And you can kind of imagine from

00:49:20.520 --> 00:49:25.080
the name what that is. It's sort of like a
hostile takeover, but with a lot of guys with

00:49:25.080 --> 00:49:30.670
guns and with an extremely murky can sense
of, you know, kind of corporate rules grafted

00:49:30.670 --> 00:49:34.080
on top of it.
That problem, believe it or not, is worse

00:49:34.080 --> 00:49:38.460
in Ukraine now than it was under Yanukovych.
And it was extremely bad. It was a tens of

00:49:38.460 --> 00:49:43.690
billions of dollar scale problem under Yanukovych.
It's worse now in part because everybody's

00:49:43.690 --> 00:49:49.300
fighting over the spoils of what Yanukovych
and the family left behind. But more so because

00:49:49.300 --> 00:49:53.170
the irony is, at least under Yanukovych you
had a kind of monopoly on force. Now you have

00:49:53.170 --> 00:49:58.520
private armies. Which means either the oligarchs
are literally sending their private—their

00:49:58.520 --> 00:50:03.411
boys with guns to take stuff that belongs
to someone else that they want, or, they're

00:50:03.411 --> 00:50:07.270
just for hire. You just have a lot of people
with a lot of guns, and you have a general

00:50:07.270 --> 00:50:11.680
atmosphere that lacks any real sense of rule
of law. This is on a high level, but this

00:50:11.680 --> 00:50:15.290
is also on a low level.
So, you know, what do you do about that? Well,

00:50:15.290 --> 00:50:19.720
it's a huge problem; right? This isn't something
you flip a switch, you know, in an IMF program,

00:50:19.720 --> 00:50:24.130
click, you know, nine months later we're done,
right? This is a deep-seated kind of—here's

00:50:24.130 --> 00:50:28.970
one way I put it. When, you know, Sasha, the
Ukrainian businessman cannot wait to drive

00:50:28.970 --> 00:50:33.440
home to complain to his wife about the corrupt
official who's trying to, you know, extort

00:50:33.440 --> 00:50:37.260
a bribe from him at work and he gets stopped
by a traffic cop because he's speeding a little

00:50:37.260 --> 00:50:42.080
bit; when his instinct stops being 'slip the
guy 500&nbsp;hryvnia so he can get home faster

00:50:42.080 --> 00:50:46.810
and keep complaining', and becomes 'I'll see
you in court, I'll follow the rules, I'll

00:50:46.810 --> 00:50:51.050
pay the fine', et cetera, et cetera—in other
words, a bottom-up change—then we have different

00:50:51.050 --> 00:50:54.670
Ukraine. We're very, very far from that.
In the short term, some simple things you

00:50:54.670 --> 00:50:59.610
could do; right? The Rada has already passed
a reform package. But at the last minute—and

00:50:59.610 --> 00:51:03.240
this doesn't surprise me at all, given who's
in this Rada and the fact that, you know,

00:51:03.240 --> 00:51:07.130
a huge proportion—I won't specify how many
because I don't know—are very much under

00:51:07.130 --> 00:51:11.320
the sway of the most powerful and now well-armed
oligarchs in the country.

00:51:11.320 --> 00:51:16.540
They stripped out the criminal penalties for
the most critical pieces of this&nbsp;reform package,

00:51:16.540 --> 00:51:20.800
including the law on beneficial ownership.
It's a very important law, because it says

00:51:20.800 --> 00:51:24.990
who owns what. Otherwise, everything's held
by a hundred different layers of shell corporations,

00:51:24.990 --> 00:51:28.070
and you don't even know who owns something
to begin with, right?

00:51:28.070 --> 00:51:34.630
This is not beside the point, guys. Violence
in Ukraine, chaos, corruption, it all happens

00:51:34.630 --> 00:51:39.141
over who gets to own and control the sources
of national wealth. I mean, Ambassador Herbst

00:51:39.141 --> 00:51:42.500
knows this story very well from his time as
ambassador. So that's the first thing you

00:51:42.500 --> 00:51:45.950
fix.
The other thing you fix is, you have a vision

00:51:45.950 --> 00:51:49.420
for getting the guns out of the hands of the
people who shouldn't have them, and for getting

00:51:49.420 --> 00:51:55.420
some kind of national project, some kind of
something in the way of national reconciliation,

00:51:55.420 --> 00:51:59.300
so that people who live in one part of the
country don't feel like they have to raise

00:51:59.300 --> 00:52:04.300
private militias and rely on these local kingpins—warlords.
Warlords are the right term. We should call

00:52:04.300 --> 00:52:08.030
them warlords—in order to defend them against
people in another part of the country.

00:52:08.030 --> 00:52:12.050
And I'm not only talking about Donbass versus
the rest of Ukraine here. I wish it was that

00:52:12.050 --> 00:52:16.700
clean a division. It's nothing even remotely
like that. So the scale of this problem is&nbsp;actually

00:52:16.700 --> 00:52:21.190
so enormous, Anya, that this isn't the kind
of thing that, you know, the IMF experts are

00:52:21.190 --> 00:52:26.190
going to just write up a list and&nbsp;Yatsenyuk&nbsp;and
his emergency cabinet meeting last night can,

00:52:26.190 --> 00:52:31.670
you know, adopt all of these things as new
law, sign the papers, and they get $17 billion

00:52:31.670 --> 00:52:34.880
and it's done. It's nothing even remotely
like that. I'm sorry.

00:52:34.880 --> 00:52:42.310
DEYOUNG: Did you --
HERBST: I'll answer much more quickly. The

00:52:42.310 --> 00:52:49.580
differences in Ukrainian population between
east and west is much less than many commentators

00:52:49.580 --> 00:52:55.670
have expected and assumed. And while there's
a need for a certain amount of autonomy in

00:52:55.670 --> 00:53:01.590
the east, especially in the Donbass if Ukraine
ever reestablishes control over the Donbass,

00:53:01.590 --> 00:53:07.510
that is a manageable problem.
The problem of overall reform of Ukraine is

00:53:07.510 --> 00:53:14.150
an enormous issue. And it's as important as
defending Ukraine against the Kremlin's aggression.

00:53:14.150 --> 00:53:19.950
On reform, you certainly have the best government
and the best Rada in Ukraine's history to

00:53:19.950 --> 00:53:27.500
address this. You certainly have at the lower
policy level and among many Rada members legitimate

00:53:27.500 --> 00:53:32.420
reformers. And that's wonderful, for the first
time in Ukraine's history.

00:53:32.420 --> 00:53:37.601
It's also true that Poroshenko and&nbsp;Yatsenyuk
represent the best leadership that the country's

00:53:37.601 --> 00:53:43.250
ever had to address reform. But it's also
true that they may not be good enough. I know

00:53:43.250 --> 00:53:48.600
them well. I think they both understand that
reform is important. I think they're also

00:53:48.600 --> 00:53:53.350
both used to dealing in the back rooms and
in the corrupt ways that have been critical

00:53:53.350 --> 00:53:58.420
to success in the country in the past.
I give Poroshenko reasonably high marks as

00:53:58.420 --> 00:54:03.490
a national security leader dealing with this
Kremlin aggression. I would give him at best

00:54:03.490 --> 00:54:09.310
a C-minus as a reform leader. I wouldn't give
him an F. Because, in fact, the --

00:54:09.310 --> 00:54:11.320
(CROSSTALK)
HERBST: Well, no. Because the legislation

00:54:11.320 --> 00:54:15.070
put before the Rada was not bad. It represents
an improvement, even with the problems that

00:54:15.070 --> 00:54:20.160
you referred to. The point is, however, it's
not good enough, in my judgment.

00:54:20.160 --> 00:54:27.000
I think that the West has a policy of tough
love on this subject, which is not followed

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:33.430
sufficiently closely. I think that the—it
was not a coincidence, as the Soviets used

00:54:33.430 --> 00:54:38.280
to say, that Christine Lagarde offered the
IMF package as Poroshenko was wavering on

00:54:38.280 --> 00:54:43.680
signing Minsk Two, which was a disgrace as
a document if you look at the things put on

00:54:43.680 --> 00:54:47.970
Ukraine.
So I think for reasons of sympathy on the

00:54:47.970 --> 00:54:53.110
security side, the IMF and the West has not
been as tough on the reform side as it should

00:54:53.110 --> 00:55:00.020
be. And that's a problem. So to my mind, coming
back to Matt's need to have a policy which

00:55:00.020 --> 00:55:04.412
encompasses everything, the West should be
much tougher on the security issues in the

00:55:04.412 --> 00:55:09.090
ways I've described. But also tougher on the
reform side. This will be the fastest path

00:55:09.090 --> 00:55:14.250
both to contain a revisionist Mr. Putin and
putting Ukraine on the path which will have

00:55:14.250 --> 00:55:21.870
it developing—I agree with Matt, not as
nicely and as neatly as Poland, but along

00:55:21.870 --> 00:55:25.360
that track.
DEYOUNG: We only have a few more minutes.

00:55:25.360 --> 00:55:30.440
And so why don't we take two or three questions
and just give your questions in a row. And

00:55:30.440 --> 00:55:38.180
then I'll turn the floor over to our two speakers.
Yes, sir.

00:55:38.180 --> 00:55:43.960
QUESTION:&nbsp;Hans Binnendijk from SAIS and&nbsp;Rand.
So I'd like to follow up on the question about

00:55:43.960 --> 00:55:50.080
Germany and Mariupol.
A number of conservative German politicians

00:55:50.080 --> 00:55:56.120
close to Chancellor Merkel have been talking
about what you might call the Mariupol test.

00:55:56.120 --> 00:56:02.260
That is, if the Russians break out of the
current area in which they're in and we see

00:56:02.260 --> 00:56:08.200
an effort to take Mariupol, that would be
the trigger for both their support for the

00:56:08.200 --> 00:56:14.180
provision of defensive lethal weapons, but
also for an increase in economic sanctions.

00:56:14.180 --> 00:56:20.990
And that kind of an approach would tend to
bring together the allies, rather than divide

00:56:20.990 --> 00:56:27.550
them, and would also have the—would contain,
rather than try to roll back, which is very

00:56:27.550 --> 00:56:32.650
hard to do as much as I'd like to. So just
give me your thoughts on why that wouldn't

00:56:32.650 --> 00:56:39.080
work.
DEYOUNG: And do we have one more? Yes.

00:56:39.080 --> 00:56:43.700
QUESTION: Jason Rockett, Greenmantle. The
one side of Russia that we haven't really

00:56:43.700 --> 00:56:50.360
spoken about is the eastern side. And historically,
the U.S. has used its relationship with China

00:56:50.360 --> 00:56:56.310
and Japan to put pressure on Russia in cases
where Russia was applying pressure on Europe.

00:56:56.310 --> 00:57:00.940
We haven't really seen any of that in the
last year. And I was wondering what kind of

00:57:00.940 --> 00:57:04.590
practical and realistic optionss do we have
there with our allies?

00:57:04.590 --> 00:57:18.760
DEYOUNG: OK. Who would like go first on that?
ROJANSKY: Sure. OK. Just on—yes. On Mariupol

00:57:18.760 --> 00:57:23.370
test. If I understood your actual question
at the end correctly, you're saying why wouldn't,

00:57:23.370 --> 00:57:27.930
then, additional sanctions and weapons work
at that point?

00:57:27.930 --> 00:57:31.770
QUESTION: Do you support the idea?
ROJANSKY: Do I support the idea. So my answer

00:57:31.770 --> 00:57:37.240
to that is basically—so I think your analysis
of the Mariupol test is probably right. I

00:57:37.240 --> 00:57:41.310
think that's an accurate diagnosis of European
and American politics at this moment. Which

00:57:41.310 --> 00:57:44.520
is part of why I said for now, it's kind of
moot in watching them. But that's only for

00:57:44.520 --> 00:57:48.880
now. If we get to that, if think you're right,
we'll have transatlantic unity.

00:57:48.880 --> 00:57:52.470
I think you can put the line wherever you
want to put it. You can put it at Mariupol.

00:57:52.470 --> 00:57:57.270
If you want to put it another hundred kilometers
in, you know, pick another target, Sloviansk,

00:57:57.270 --> 00:58:03.810
you can do that too. My question is still
going to be—and to get me onboard, whatever

00:58:03.810 --> 00:58:07.520
the policy is, is going to be OK, what then?
What is the vision?

00:58:07.520 --> 00:58:11.520
So if the vision is, so then we're going to
send X amount of weapons, then I actually

00:58:11.520 --> 00:58:16.880
agree with Ambassador Herbst; A, you need
to preserve genuine ambiguity within the threat

00:58:16.880 --> 00:58:22.180
so that it's a real threat. But you have to
know why it is that you're prepared to escalate

00:58:22.180 --> 00:58:26.810
to that level; to X amount of weapons, to
X-plus-ten amount. And then to whatever it

00:58:26.810 --> 00:58:32.430
might take to get ultimately where you probably
need to go—and this is where I sort of hedgingly

00:58:32.430 --> 00:58:38.630
and cautiously probably also agree with Ambassador
Herbst—that Putin probably isn't going to

00:58:38.630 --> 00:58:41.160
stop. He probably will look to open another
front.

00:58:41.160 --> 00:58:44.800
And if that's the case, you really have to
be committed. And if you're committed, you

00:58:44.800 --> 00:58:49.190
have to know why you're committed and think
about where it ends. If it does end with us

00:58:49.190 --> 00:58:54.110
being in a proxy war or a real war with the
Russians. If it does end with scenarios where

00:58:54.110 --> 00:58:59.710
we are looking to undercut the Putin regime
so much, including, you know, backing internal

00:58:59.710 --> 00:59:04.790
opposition to the hilt, taking him out, whatever
those strategies might look like, this is—you

00:59:04.790 --> 00:59:07.670
see, I'm saying—so this is scary stuff,
right?

00:59:07.670 --> 00:59:11.790
But we're through the looking glass here.
Once we declare—what I don't want to do

00:59:11.790 --> 00:59:16.700
is I don't want to declare an American line
and then answer that with either nothing or

00:59:16.700 --> 00:59:21.380
with something that is very clearly inadequate
that he—you know, he says I see your rockets—not

00:59:21.380 --> 00:59:25.930
missiles. Sorry—I see your rockets and I
raise you more rockets.

00:59:25.930 --> 00:59:29.100
That's the scenario I don't want to be in,
right? So if we follow through, we have to

00:59:29.100 --> 00:59:34.000
know why we're following through and where
it ends. And I would hope, in fact, Europe

00:59:34.000 --> 00:59:38.830
is in the lead in that conversation. Not because
I'm afraid of American leadership. I agree

00:59:38.830 --> 00:59:42.050
on every rhetorical and political level. And
if I were writing speeches for the President,

00:59:42.050 --> 00:59:45.800
I'd write them very differently. But because
I think Europe has a much better sense of

00:59:45.800 --> 00:59:50.610
where it ends, and why it matters where it
ends. You know, our neighbors are Canada,

00:59:50.610 --> 00:59:56.540
Mexico, and fish. Their neighbors are Russia.
They know how this stuff works.

00:59:56.540 --> 01:00:01.800
And then just on China—not a China expert.
The one thing that I have observed—you know,

01:00:01.800 --> 01:00:05.369
obviously, we've got—we have Japan and South
Korea to the extent that we were going to

01:00:05.369 --> 01:00:11.860
have them. It's always going to be less than
we want and slower than we want. But the problem

01:00:11.860 --> 01:00:16.180
that I see with China—I talk to a lot of
Chinese journalists much more so lately because

01:00:16.180 --> 01:00:20.010
they're always very eager to know what are
we going to do in terms of sanctions.

01:00:20.010 --> 01:00:24.450
My sense is not only, you know, is there this
sort of&nbsp;schadenfreude that the Chinese are

01:00:24.450 --> 01:00:30.231
thrilled to see this Russia-West, you know,
partnership come apart at the seams. But there's

01:00:30.231 --> 01:00:36.430
also a sense that the Chinese are the first
major global economy to begin hedging against

01:00:36.430 --> 01:00:41.030
the Western-dominated global financial and
trading system, that they look at the sanctions

01:00:41.030 --> 01:00:45.740
and they read it a little differently than
we do. We say Russia has broken the rules

01:00:45.740 --> 01:00:50.280
and so Russia is outside the system and so
Russia has to be punished.

01:00:50.280 --> 01:00:54.690
They look at it and they say no, no, no, the
rule was this was going to be an apolitical

01:00:54.690 --> 01:00:59.210
system that was going to be a level playing
field for all of us. You, the West, have violated

01:00:59.210 --> 01:01:04.480
that rule by making it political. So we are
going to, on the margins at least, shift resources

01:01:04.480 --> 01:01:09.170
out of the system and try to come up with
an alternative. That's—it's just one more

01:01:09.170 --> 01:01:15.830
thing we have to take into account.
HERBST: OK. I'll start with China. I'm not

01:01:15.830 --> 01:01:21.970
sure that there's much of a card for us to
play with China. Because China's actually

01:01:21.970 --> 01:01:26.420
one of the beneficiaries of this current crisis.
Whether it's because Russia may be willing

01:01:26.420 --> 01:01:33.360
to sell them gas at a reduced price or it's
because as Russia tears up the international

01:01:33.360 --> 01:01:39.740
system, China sees precedence it can use in
the seas nearby. So I don't think they'd be

01:01:39.740 --> 01:01:46.890
a balancer for us against&nbsp;Mr. Putin.
Regarding Hans' point, I think the Mariupol

01:01:46.890 --> 01:01:53.040
test would be wonderful. I think that would
finally have Europe setting down clear lines

01:01:53.040 --> 01:01:59.790
and letting Mr. Putin know in advance that
he pays a higher price for aggression. And

01:01:59.790 --> 01:02:03.470
I agree as well with Matt's point that it
need not be Mariupol, it can be somewhere

01:02:03.470 --> 01:02:05.730
else.
Because again, for reasons I provided for

01:02:05.730 --> 01:02:13.270
analysis—per analysis I provided, Mr. Putin's
not going to stop. So he will run up against

01:02:13.270 --> 01:02:18.540
that line. And that will begin to establish,
in Europe as well as hopefully in Washington,

01:02:18.540 --> 01:02:22.850
the strong policies we need to deal with him.
Hopefully in the Ukraine. But if not in Ukraine,

01:02:22.850 --> 01:02:30.280
then beyond Ukraine.
And I don't share Matt's view that it'll be

01:02:30.280 --> 01:02:35.300
a national catastrophe and loss of American
prestige if we provide weapons and Mr. Putin

01:02:35.300 --> 01:02:39.420
nonetheless conquers parts of Ukraine. We've
done that in the past and we've let people

01:02:39.420 --> 01:02:43.220
know in the past yes, we are willing to provide
weapons for conflict X, but not put American

01:02:43.220 --> 01:02:47.130
soldiers on the ground.
But as part of a long game to keep Mr. Putin

01:02:47.130 --> 01:02:52.230
in check, it's a smart move. And the downside
is relatively small. In fact, the downside

01:02:52.230 --> 01:02:55.800
really doesn't exist. Only in the minds of
people who are overly cautious and don't want

01:02:55.800 --> 01:03:00.220
to see the real danger.
ROJANSKY: Wait. No—no, I have to disagree

01:03:00.220 --> 01:03:02.360
with that. Because --
DEYOUNG: Briefly, because we're really out

01:03:02.360 --> 01:03:03.470
out of time.
ROJANSKY: Because I think the downside is

01:03:03.470 --> 01:03:07.960
for Ukraine, right? So—so Anya had the right
question about what happens to Ukraine. And

01:03:07.960 --> 01:03:12.870
the downside, if all we get is a massive upsurge
in fighting and Putin wins anyway and Ukraine

01:03:12.870 --> 01:03:16.510
remains dysfunctional anyway is we end up
with 50,000 more dead Ukrainians. That's a

01:03:16.510 --> 01:03:19.040
pretty big downside.
HERBST: But the Ukrainians want it. They're

01:03:19.040 --> 01:03:21.690
asking for it.
ROJANSKY: Well, again, that's to my point.

01:03:21.690 --> 01:03:25.869
With all due respect to my Ukrainian friends,
you know, that may not mean that we should

01:03:25.869 --> 01:03:29.350
give it to them.
HERBST:&nbsp;...father knows best.

01:03:29.350 --> 01:03:34.150
DEYOUNG: I think we're all going to have occasion
to be back here and continue this conversation.

01:03:34.150 --> 01:03:37.090
So thank you all very much. And particularly,
thank you so much to our speakers.

01:03:37.090 --> 01:03:37.330
END

